.TH KEYSRV 4
.SH NAME
keysrv \- secret key server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/keysrv
[
.BI -k " keyfile"
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Keysrv
is a file service run on a connection to an authentication server.
It allows a remote user
to change a secret stored on the server by
.IR keyfs (4),
which must have been started before
.IR keysrv ,
in a name space with the authentication data available under
.BR /mnt/keys .
.PP
.I Keysrv
serves a single file,
.BR secret ,
on a connection accessed through file descriptor 0 (ie, the standard `input').
When invoked, it
first authenticates the connection using
.IR security-auth (2),
requiring the client to use
.B aes_256_gcm
.RI ( ssl (3)).
It authenticates itself with the signer's own key,
.B /keydb/signerkey
(or
.IR keyfile ),
so that a client can tell it is the signer's key server and not
another holder of one of the signer's certificates:
the name in that key is certified for nobody else
(see
.IR logind (8)).
If authentication succeeds,
.I keysrv
exports a name space containing a file
.BR secret .
The authentication ensures that only a user that possesses a valid certificate can connect to the service.
.PP
The authenticated user (ie, the user name in the verified certificate)
must be one account name as
.IR login (6)
defines it, have an entry in
.BR /mnt/keys ,
as served by
.IR keyfs (4),
and have a non-empty secret;
otherwise every read or write returns an appropriate error.
.PP
A read of
.B secret
returns a challenge:
.IP
.EX
keysrv 2 \fIchallenge\fP
.EE
.PP
where
.I challenge
is 32 random bytes as 64 hexadecimal digits.
There is a new one after every write.
A write answers the challenge last read:
.IP
.EX
.fi
.I proof
[
.I newsecret
]
.EE
.PP
With
.I secret
the user's existing secret as
.IR keyfs (4)
holds it (the SHA-256 of the password),
.IP
.EX
PRK = HMAC-SHA-384(challenge, secret)
proof = HKDF-Expand(PRK, "infernode keysrv 2 proof" || user, 48)
key || nonce = HKDF-Expand(PRK,
    "infernode keysrv 2 new secret" || user, 44)
newsecret = AES-256-GCM(key, nonce, SHA-256(new password))
.EE
.PP
.I Proof
is 96 hexadecimal digits.
.I Newsecret
is the ciphertext and its tag (the associated data is the label
.BR "infernode keysrv 2 new secret" ),
96 hexadecimal digits,
separated from
.I proof
by a single space.
If
.I proof
is right for the secret currently stored and
.I newsecret
is given and opens to 32 bytes, they replace the stored secret;
they are what
.IR login (6)
expects there.
A write of
.I proof
alone checks the secret and changes nothing.
The write returns an error if
.I proof
is wrong
.RB ( "wrong secret" ),
or if something else goes wrong.
.PP
A proof is good for one challenge, so what a client sent cannot be sent again;
the proof and the new secret say nothing to anyone without the old secret,
though whoever is sent them could try passwords against them, which is why the
client first makes sure the server is the signer.
Before version 2 the proof was a hash of the stored secret, the same every time,
and the new secret was sent and stored as typed, which no login then matched.
.PP
Wrong proofs are guesses at the password, and are counted where
.IR logind (8)
counts logins that do not prove it,
.BR /keydb/loginfail :
ten in a row for a name, here or there, and both refuse the name for a minute.
A session takes five wrong proofs and then no more of any kind.
When the audit log is set up
.RI ( auditfs (4)),
.I keysrv
records
.B authfail
for a wrong proof,
.B authlock
for a refusal and
.B secretchange
for a secret changed.
.PP
.I Keysrv
can be invoked via
.IR listen (1):
.IP
.EX
listen -t -A 'tcp!*!infkey' {auth/keysrv}
.EE
.PP
Normally that is done automatically when
starting an authentication service using
.B svc/auth
(see
.IR svc (8)).
.PP
.IR Passwd (1)
dials the service, authenticates, and mounts the resulting connection on
.BR /mnt/keysrv ,
where it accesses the
.B secret
file to change the secret.
.SH FILES
.TF /mnt/keysrv
.TP
.B /mnt/keys
mount point for
.IR keyfs (4)
.TP
.B /mnt/keysrv
exported mount point for
.I keysrv
.TP
.B /keydb/signerkey
the signer's key, with which
.I keysrv
authenticates
.TP
.B /keydb/loginfail
counts of failed attempts, shared with
.IR logind (8)
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/keysrv.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR listen (1),
.IR passwd (1),
.IR keyfs (4),
.IR login (6),
.IR logind (8)
